            <ArticleTitle>Primary sacral lymphoma: a case report and review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>659-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5137/1019-5149.JTN .3001-10.1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Primary sacral lymphoma is rare. It usually presents in elderly males as low back ache and radiculopathy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">We hereby report a case of primary sacral lymphoma in a 53-year-old male who presented with low back pain. Imaging showed a sacral lesion which on histopathology proved to be a non-Hodgkin's lymphoma of B cell lineage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The case is presented for its rarity and for the reason that it mimicked a primary bone tumor. Its recognition is important because it has an excellent prognosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Primary sacral epidural lymphoma should be considered in the differential diagnosis of a sacral mass.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
